Z||000000||Jsears8||11-23-2001||10:25 PM||||Whats the best and easiest way to do a clutch swap on my '88 Fiero 2.5?  Any online install pages?  Also what kind of clutch would you recommend as a replacement?<P>Thanks,<BR>Justin <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/jsears8" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/jsears8</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||11-23-2001||10:48 PM||||Well, you just asked one of the hottest questions on this forum.  Try a search.  You'll find a few opinions on clutches and lots of people telling you to do a search.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><P>Anyway, check out Ed Park's Fiero Factory for info on how to change the clutch without removing the engine. ( <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#trany" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#trany</A>  )  I've done it in my 88 Formula V6, and while it's a big job, it's not beyond the backyard mechanic with a good set of hand tools, a couple floor jacks, some jackstands, and at least one friend to help. <P>Good luck!  <P>Oh, I've currently got the Ram clutch from the Fiero Store.  ( <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierostore.com</A>  )  It's not bad, but some people have had bad luck with them.  For your 4-banger, you could probably just get an OEM replacement from you local auto parts store and be fine. ||||reg||3
Z||000002||Jsears8||11-25-2001||02:06 AM||||Thanks a bunch man!  Going to have to tackle this one day.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
